import{_ as e,o,c as a,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.BM5YPQmL.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"OCBL: dual-firmware boot and A/B slot decisions","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"components/ocbl/ocbl-ab.md","filePath":"components/ocbl/ocbl-ab.md"}'),d={name:"components/ocbl/ocbl-ab.md"};function s(i,t,n,h,l,c){return o(),a("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[r('<h1 id="ocbl-dual-firmware-boot-and-a-b-slot-decisions" tabindex="-1">OCBL: dual-firmware boot and A/B slot decisions <a class="header-anchor" href="#ocbl-dual-firmware-boot-and-a-b-slot-decisions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;OCBL: dual-firmware boot and A/B slot decisions&quot;">​</a></h1><p>How OCBL picks what to boot, how the A/B slots on the recovery disk are judged, and what the two low-memory state areas mean. The contract is shared byte-for-byte by three independent builds - OCBL (<code>ocbl/</code>), the OCOS kernel (<code>src/L0/</code>) and the OCIRE recovery kernel (<code>ocire/</code>) - in <code>include/ocbl_state.h</code>.</p><h2 id="the-a-b-recovery-disk-two-independent-physical-partitions" tabindex="-1">The A/B recovery disk: two independent physical partitions <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-a-b-recovery-disk-two-independent-physical-partitions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The A/B recovery disk: two independent physical partitions&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The WP-07 recovery disk carries OCIRE in <strong>two independent physical partitions</strong>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Slot</th><th>Partition</th><th>Start LBA</th><th>Size</th><th>Filesystem</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>OCIRE-A</td><td>p1</td><td>2048</td><td>64 MiB</td><td>real FAT32, LFN read support</td></tr><tr><td>OCIRE-B</td><td>p2</td><td>133120</td><td>64 MiB</td><td>real FAT32, LFN read support</td></tr></tbody></table><p>This is a genuine two-partition layout - not one partition holding two image files. The older FAT12 0xEE shadow-volume media still mounts (compatibility); on the current media the shadow is retired.</p><p>The slot selection block is the <strong>A/B flags sector at LBA 1024</strong>, between the OCBL image and the first partition - a 24-byte <code>OCIREAB1</code> structure whose layout OCBL and OCIRE share byte-for-byte:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>magic[8]</code></td><td><code>&quot;OCIREAB1&quot;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>active_slot</code></td><td>1 = A, 2 = B (the last confirmed-good slot)</td></tr><tr><td><code>try_slot</code></td><td>0 = none; otherwise the candidate being tried</td></tr><tr><td><code>attempts</code></td><td>boot attempts made on <code>try_slot</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>flags</code></td><td>bit 0 = dirty</td></tr><tr><td><code>boot_count</code></td><td>successful OCIRE boots</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="slot-decision-flow" tabindex="-1">Slot decision flow <a class="header-anchor" href="#slot-decision-flow" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Slot decision flow&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li>OCBL reads the flags sector and picks the slot: <code>try_slot</code> if one is being tried, otherwise <code>active_slot</code>.</li><li>It bumps <code>attempts</code> for the candidate <strong>before</strong> loading it.</li><li>If the candidate will not load, OCBL falls back to the other slot.</li><li>A slot that runs is <strong>confirmed by OCIRE</strong> (promotion): the recovery kernel updates the flags sector on a good boot, which is what makes <code>active_slot</code> honest.</li></ol><p>The OCOS A/B rollback uses the same protocol shape as the GRUB config it replaced (<code>next_B</code> / <code>ok_B</code> / <code>bootfail_B</code> on the first system volume of a disk) - the takeover menu&#39;s Rollback key flips exactly those flags.</p><h2 id="the-two-state-areas" tabindex="-1">The two state areas <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-two-state-areas" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The two state areas&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Two fixed low-memory blocks implement the boot-state monitor. Both were live-measured to survive warm resets on the reference platform (the reset wipe stops at the EBDA, 0x9FC00); a <strong>cold boot or power loss clears them</strong>, so every consumer validates the magic and the ones-complement checksum before trusting a record - no record simply means a normal boot, never a false failure.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Address</th><th>Size</th><th>Written by</th><th>Read by</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>STATE</td><td><code>0x98000</code></td><td>4 KiB</td><td>the kernel, <strong>blind</strong> (it never reads it back); OCBL re-inits at handoff</td><td>OCBL, OCIRE</td><td>what the kernel is doing right now: stage, heartbeat, progress clock</td></tr><tr><td>RESERVED</td><td><code>0x9B000</code></td><td>4 KiB</td><td>OCBL (the judgment or the user request)</td><td>OCIRE, after the reboot</td><td>the post-mortem boot record</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both pages are supervisor-only in every user address space - L1 applications cannot reach them - and both are carved out of the multiboot2 memory map as E820 reserved so no payload ever allocates them.</p><h3 id="the-boot-stages-the-state-area-s-stage-field" tabindex="-1">The boot stages (the STATE area&#39;s <code>stage</code> field) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-boot-stages-the-state-area-s-stage-field" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The boot stages (the STATE area&#39;s `stage` field)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Numeric order is boot order; the kernel only ever moves forward:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Stage</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>BOOT</td><td>pre-kmain handoff</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>KMAIN_ENTERED</td><td>kmain() entered</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>SCHED_READY</td><td>scheduler + core mechanisms</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>DRIVERS_READY</td><td>driver init complete</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>SERVICES_READY</td><td>root services ready</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>L1_LAUNCHED</td><td>default L1 app started</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>L1_READY</td><td>ush served (first syscall)</td></tr><tr><td>255</td><td>RUNTIME</td><td>marker: hung while running</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="failure-counting-and-the-clear-condition" tabindex="-1">Failure counting and the clear condition <a class="header-anchor" href="#failure-counting-and-the-clear-condition" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Failure counting and the clear condition&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>A <strong>single</strong> judged boot failure bumps the record&#39;s <code>boot_fails</code> counter and <strong>retries OCOS</strong>.</li><li>When the counter reaches <strong>2</strong> (<code>OCST_BOOT_FAILS_ROUTE</code>), OCBL auto-routes the machine into OCIRE - the takeover.</li><li>The kernel <strong>clears the counter after a confirmed good boot</strong> (L1_READY reached), so &quot;consecutive&quot; is honest: one bad boot followed by a good boot leaves the counter at zero.</li><li>The counter rides the record&#39;s former reserved byte - the record layout is unchanged, older readers keep working.</li></ul><h2 id="the-watchdog-relay-who-times-whom" tabindex="-1">The watchdog relay (who times whom) <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-watchdog-relay-who-times-whom" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The watchdog relay (who times whom)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>After the multiboot2 handoff OCBL is no longer executing, so the per-phase timeouts are enforced by the <strong>i6300ESB hardware watchdog</strong>: OCBL arms the boot budget before the handoff, the kernel re-arms the phase budget at every stage advance and pets the timer from its 1-second tick at runtime.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Budget</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>boot start -&gt; KMAIN_ENTERED</td><td>30 s (armed by OCBL pre-handoff)</td></tr><tr><td>each intermediate phase</td><td>10 s</td></tr><tr><td>L1_LAUNCHED -&gt; L1_READY</td><td>30 s</td></tr><tr><td>runtime (post L1_READY)</td><td>15 s heartbeat (1 s tick pets)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A hung kernel stops feeding the dog, the hardware resets the machine, and OCBL - running again after the reset - reads the state area, judges the stall from the stage plus the measured silence (<strong>8 s</strong> splits a manual warm reset, which measures under ~4 s of silence, from a real watchdog-caught hang, which measures at least the runtime budget), records BOOT_FAILED in the reserved area, shows the failure notice for 4 s, and pulses the 8042 reset.</p><h2 id="sleep-armed-the-wake-failure-detector" tabindex="-1">SLEEP_ARMED (the wake-failure detector) <a class="header-anchor" href="#sleep-armed-the-wake-failure-detector" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;SLEEP_ARMED (the wake-failure detector)&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The kernel sets the <code>SLEEP_ARMED</code> flag immediately before the SLP_EN write (entering S3/S0ix) and clears it on the resume path. A reboot that finds the flag still set means the wake vector crashed or the machine died mid-sleep: OCBL records WAKE_FAILED and routes OCIRE, which offers the wake-failure takeover menu.</p><p>Next: <a href="./ocbl-troubleshooting.html">troubleshooting and status codes</a>.</p>',27)])])}const m=e(d,[["render",s]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
